00:00:06.349 --> 00:00:08.330 align:start position:0%
this is Lebanon's day of anger thousands  of protestors determined to force their  way to the country's parliament building  their route blocked by riot police  standing behind barbed wire barricades  hurling rocks sticks and pretty much  anything else they could get their hands  on demonstrators try to push the police  back and this was the response water  cannon  and volley after volley of teargas  ambulances rushed hundreds of injured to  nearby hospitals while security forces  dragged demonstrators away others had to  take shelter in a nearby mosque one of  this country's most iconic buildings  turned into a makeshift clinic  for over three months downtown Beirut  has been the epicenter of a nationwide  movement calling for the removal of the  entire political establishment the  country's leadership has promised reform  but so far delivered nothing  transforming 94 days of what has been  overwhelmingly peaceful protests into  this a crippling economic crisis has  seen jobs lost to people's salaries cuts  and medical supplies running short  Lebanon's financial institutions have  borne the brunt of people's anger banks  have kept cash withdrawals to just a few  hundred pounds a month leaving many  unable to pay rent school fees and  hospital bills  [Applause]  Lebanon is in crisis it's politicians  seemingly incapable of addressing their  people's concerns they'll need to do so  quickly or risk watching this country  collapse into an even more dangerous  cycle of violence  Sanja for Sky News Beirut  [Music]
 

